<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanoi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanoi is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> on the Red River, the historic centre of Vietnamese states. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> it became the seat of the governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>. It was the administrative hub from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> governed its territories in South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanoi.jpg" alt="Hanoi" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quangnlnhe182394 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanoi_skyline_with_Ba_Vi_Mountain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hanoi lies on the Red River, whose curved course around the city was symbolised as a dragon. Several of the city's older names contain the Chinese word for dragon: it was first called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-bien">Long Biên</a>, then Tống Bình and Long Đỗ, and after its recapture in 866 by the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-pian">Gao Pian</a> it was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai-la">Đại La</a>. Medieval Muslim sources between the 9th and 12th century called it Luqin, a term derived from Longbian.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a> of the Sơn Vi culture, 10,000 to 20,000 years old, have been found at Ba Vì and Đông Anh. From about 10,000 to 4,000 years ago the area was under water as the sea rose, and the region is believed to have been inhabited continuously for the last 4,000 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around the third century BCE a fortified citadel, later named Cổ Loa, was built in the area; it was the first political centre of the Vietnamese civilisation before Chinese influence, with an outer embankment covering 600 hectares. In 179 BCE Nanyue annexed the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a> left the indigenous chiefs in control of the population. For the first time the area formed part of a polity headed by a Chinese ruler.</p><p>In 1010, under the Lý dynasty, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-thai-to">Lý Thái Tổ</a> established the capital of Đại Việt in what later became central Hanoi and named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, meaning ascending dragon. In 1428 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> renamed it Đông Kinh, eastern capital, a name used until 1789. In 1802 the Nguyễn dynasty moved the national capital to Huế, and the city was renamed Hanoi in 1831. The French took control in 1888 and shaped the city's architecture to their tastes. Hanoi was the capital of the French protectorate of Tonkin from 1883 to 1949 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> from 1902 to 1945. Ernest Hebrard drew up a development plan in 1924, which was partly carried out.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a> and the fall of the Nguyễn dynasty, the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> made Hanoi its capital. The city was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> from 1949 to 1954 and again of the Democratic Republic from 1954 to 1976. In 1976 it became the capital of the unified Socialist Republic of Vietnam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hanoi is the capital and second most populous centrally governed city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, with about 8.7 million people. Its Old Quarter, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoan-kiem-lake">Hoàn Kiếm Lake</a>, keeps most of its original street layout, descended from the 36 streets where merchants specialised in trades such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, jewellery or bamboo. The city also holds the Imperial Citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, the Ho Chi Minh Mausoleum and the French colonial Hanoi Opera House and St. Joseph's Cathedral. UNESCO gave Hanoi the title City for Peace on 16 July 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hanoi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Soon after Japan surrendered, the Việt Minh entered <b>Hanoi</b> in the August Revolution, and on 2 September 1945 Hồ Chí Minh declared the independence of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam, replacing the Nguyễn dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Indochina was administered as five distinct territories under a governor-general, based in <b>Hanoi</b> from 1902.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Paul Doumer, who built Indochina’s federal government and the <b>Hanoi</b> bridge first named after him, later became President of France and was assassinated in 1932.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The names of Lê-era doctoral graduates were carved on stone stelae resting on turtles at <b>Hanoi</b>'s Temple of Literature from 1484; dozens of these stelae still stand there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Nguyen Anh captured Phu Xuan in 1801, proclaimed the reign name Gia Long in 1802 and took Thang Long (<b>Hanoi</b>) the same year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…more than 100,000 men to guerrilla tactics, but a canal built during the defeat supplied a second attack that took the coastal lands around Guangzhou and the provinces of Fuzhou and Guilin, perhaps reaching <b>Hanoi</b>; more than 100,000 prisoners and exiles were then moved to colonise the conquests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
